<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Fun Outdoors! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like to Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow two-step spoken instructions related to classroom activities. Interactive: Retell a simple instruction or message accurately to another peer.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow two-step instructions related to classroom outdoor activities (e.g., "Run to the corner and pick up the ball."). Can listen to simple instructions and retell them to a peer (e.g., "Pass the ball and jump three times."). Can get the gist of a 2-line conversation (e.g., "Do you want to play soccer, Maria?" "Yes, I love soccer").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Listen and Do!" TPR activity. Teacher says action words related to outdoor activities (e.g., "Run!", "Jump!", "Swim!", "Play soccer!") and students perform the actions. Teacher uses large, clear flashcards for each action. Students listen to the word and react with their bodies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary using flashcards and exaggerated actions. For example, the teacher shows a flashcard of a child running and says, "Run! I run fast." (stressing /r/ sound). Then shows a child swimming, "Swim! I swim slowly." (stressing /s/ sound). Teacher also models "play soccer" using a picture of a ball and acting out kicking. Teacher points to "game" flashcard and says G /g/ "game".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the introduced vocabulary.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a flashcard of "swim" and asks, "Can you run in the water?" (Expects head shake or "No."). "What can you do in the water?" (Expects students to mimic swimming or point to "swim" flashcard).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a flashcard of "fast" and "slow" (e.g., a rabbit and a turtle). Teacher says, "Is this fast or slow?" (pointing to rabbit). "Is this fast or slow?" (pointing to turtle). Students listen and point to the correct picture.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue with corresponding picture flashcards/storyboard depicting children talking about their favorite outdoor activities. (e.g., "Do you want to play soccer, Maria?" "Yes, I love soccer! It's fun.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing preferences for activities: "I like to run. It's fun." Asking questions about others' preferences: "Do you like to play outside?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher plays a short audio dialogue. Then asks: "What activities do the children like?" (Students listen and point to flashcards of "soccer," "swimming," "running" on the board).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Thumbs Up/Down):</b> Teacher plays a sentence from the audio (e.g., "Maria loves soccer."). Teacher then says, "Maria loves swimming. True or false?" Students show thumbs up for true or thumbs down for false.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher plays a sentence (e.g., "Juan likes to run fast."). Students listen and point to the flashcard showing "running fast."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Subject + verb + adverb (e.g., "I run fast." "I swim slowly."), Present simple (e.g., "I love to run."), WH-questions (e.g., "Why do you like to swim?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: run, jump, swim, play, soccer, ball, fast, slow, happy, fun, park, activity.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: /r/ in "run", /s/ in "swim", G /g/ in "game".</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice – "Listen and Point":</b> Teacher says a sentence, and students point to the corresponding flashcard or perform a simple action. E.g., Teacher says: "I like to *swim* slowly." Students point to the 'swim' flashcard and move their arms slowly. Teacher says: "He runs *fast* in the park." Students point to the 'run fast' flashcard and pretend to run. This practices listening for verbs and adverbs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – "Listen and Choose the Sound":</b> Teacher presents two flashcards (e.g., "run" and "swim"). Teacher says one of the words, emphasizing the initial sound /r/ or /s/. Students listen and touch the correct flashcard. Teacher then says a word like "game" and asks "What's the first sound?" encouraging students to identify /g/ and point to the letter G.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/Peer Check – "Listen and Follow My Friend":</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher gives simple, two-step instructions to Student A (e.g., "Maria, jump two times and then clap your hands."). Student A listens and performs the actions. Then, the teacher gives Student B an instruction (e.g., "Juan, touch the ball and then sit down."). This practices listening and following multi-step instructions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Outdoor Activity Relay – Listen and Pass!":</b> Students form small teams (e.g., 3-4 students per team) and sit in lines. The teacher whispers a two-step instruction related to outdoor activities to the first student in each line (e.g., "Run to the door and then jump high."). The first student then whispers the instruction to the next student, who whispers it to the next, and so on. The last student in the line performs the actions. If the actions are correct, the team gets a point. The instructions incorporate target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "Swim slowly to the chair and touch it," "Play soccer quickly and then sit down," "Pass the ball to your friend and jump three times.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> The instructions are all related to "Fun Outdoors!" and "I Like to Swim." activities, reinforcing the theme in a dynamic, listening-focused way.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This activity focuses on listening for detail, accurately relaying a message (even if with gestures/minimal words), collaborative interaction within the team, and communicative task completion (performing the correct action). Fluency in understanding and relaying is prioritized.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Outdoor Activity Relay" and a final "Listen and Do" individual check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Observation:</b> Observe students' ability to follow two-step instructions and relay them accurately during the relay game.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual "Listen and Do":</b> The teacher gives each student (or small groups) one simple two-step instruction (e.g., "Touch your nose and then clap once."). Students listen and perform the actions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate understanding and execution of two-step instructions (non-verbal response).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to retell/relay the instruction to a peer (can be gestural or 1-2 words).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and collaboration in group activities.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows two pictures: one child following instructions easily (happy face) and one child looking confused (sad face). Teacher asks: "Can you listen and do the actions easily? Point to the happy face or the sad face."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Which outdoor activity do you like to listen to the most? (e.g., swim, run, jump, play soccer). Show me with a flashcard."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new action word did you hear today?" (Teacher shows flashcards for choices).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it fun to listen and play games?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Listen and Do!" warm-up effectively engaged students. The "Outdoor Activity Relay" was a great way to practice following and relaying two-step instructions. I need to ensure clear articulation of /r/, /s/, and /g/ sounds. Some students may need more one-on-one modeling for specific instructions. Next time, I will prepare more visuals for the WH-questions to make them even more accessible. The vocabulary was well-received, but I could add more varied two-step instruction combinations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today, we listened to words and instructions about outdoor fun. Next lesson, we will *read* about these fun outdoor activities and see the words written down. We will match pictures to words we read.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>